SwiftCrossUI's GTK backend, three changes to propose upstream:

1. CSS reloaded on every update: each update of a text, field or button cleared the widget's CSS
   then set it, two changes of `css`, each reloading its provider and restyling the widget, even
   with the same rules. That was half the time of every layout during a split view slide. The rules
   are now replaced in one assignment (Widget.replaceCSS), which skips identical ones. Custom
   buttons likewise reloaded their CSS and swapped their style class on every update; both now
   only change when they differ.

2. Text measurement cache: each measurement lays the text out with Pango, and layouts measure the
   same strings at the same sizes on every frame of a resize. (Labels always ellipsize at the end
   and text views never: the key says which of the two measured the text.)

diff --git a/Sources/Gtk/Widgets/Widget.swift b/Sources/Gtk/Widgets/Widget.swift
index 142e3b30..83304083 100644
--- a/Sources/Gtk/Widgets/Widget.swift
+++ b/Sources/Gtk/Widgets/Widget.swift
@@ -39,6 +39,16 @@ open class Widget: GObject {
         }
     }
 
+    /// Replaces the widget's CSS rules in one assignment, so they're only reloaded when they
+    /// changed: each change of `css` reloads its provider and restyles the widget, and clearing
+    /// then setting it directly did that twice on every update, even with the same rules.
+    public func replaceCSS(_ build: (inout CSSBlock) -> Void) {
+        var block = css
+        block.clear()
+        build(&block)
+        css = block
+    }
+
     /// A unique CSS class for this widget. The class is lazily added to the
     /// widget when this property is first accessed.
     private lazy var customCSSClass: String = {
